{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והיה אם שמוע", :translit "ve-hayah im shamo'a", :gloss "and it shall be if you hear"} {:he "אם תשמעו עד אחרית השנה", :translit "im tishme'u ad aharit ha-shanah", :gloss "if you heed until the end of the year"} {:he "יתן הגשמים בזמנם", :translit "yiten ha-geshamim bi-z'manam", :gloss "He gives the rains in their time"} {:he "ובמקום שצריך להם", :translit "u-vi-m'kom she-tzarich lahem", :gloss "and in the place where they are needed"} {:he "ואם היו צדיקים בתחלת השנה", :translit "ve-im hayu tzadikim bi-tehilat ha-shanah", :gloss "and if they were righteous at the beginning of the year"} {:he "והקב\"ה גזר עליהם גשמים וסרחו", :translit "ve-ha-kadosh baruch hu gazar aleihem geshamim ve-sarhu", :gloss "and the Holy One blessed be He decreed rains upon them and they spoiled"} {:he "מורידן במדברות ובימים", :translit "moridan ba-midbarot u-va-yamim", :gloss "He brings them down in deserts and seas"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Reshut read as im + future verb 'if you will heed,' supporting the conditional reading of the verse."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud teaches that when Israel is righteous, rain falls in its proper time and on settled land; if they sin after a decree of rain has been issued, God still fulfills the decree but causes it to fall in deserts and seas (Ta'anit 23a–23b). The phrase reshut can be read as 'if,' which supports the conditional interpretation.", :sources ["Ta'anit 23a" "Ta'anit 23b" "Deuteronomy 11:13"]}} {:quotes [{:he "בכל לבבכם ובכל נפשכם", :translit "b'khol l'vav'khem u-v'khol nafsh'khem", :gloss "with all your heart and with all your soul"} {:he "ונתתי מטר", :translit "ve-natati matar", :gloss "and I will give rain"} {:he "שאין נענין על המטר אלא עם כן יכוונו להתפלל בכל לב", :translit "she-ein ne'enin al ha-matar ela im ken yakhvenu le-hitpallel b'khol lev", :gloss "that they are not answered regarding rain unless they intend to pray with the whole heart"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Proximity read causally: the verse places 'with all your heart' immediately before 'and I will give rain,' teaching that wholehearted prayer is the precondition for the rain."}], :frame {:text "The Talmud states that rain is one of the things for which one pray specifically in the blessing of the years, and that rainfall is withheld or granted in direct response to Israel's deeds (Ta'anit 2a, 8b). The immediate sequence of 'with all your heart' followed by 'and I will give rain' (Deuteronomy 11:13–17) supports the principle that heartfelt prayer is the precondition for rainfall.", :sources ["Ta'anit 2a" "Ta'anit 8b" "Deuteronomy 11:13" "Deuteronomy 11:17"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Reshut read as im + future verb 'if you will heed,' supporting the conditional reading of the verse.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Proximity read causally: the verse places 'with all your heart' immediately before 'and I will give rain,' teaching that wholehearted prayer is the precondition for the rain.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:35:58.390021198Z", :model "glm-5.2"}